%S A401157 6,9,9,11,18,11,11,17,17,11,13,19,15,19,13,15,21,17,17,21,15,17
%N A401157 Array read by antidiagonals: T(n,k) is the wheel Ramsey number R(W_n,W_k).
%C A401157 Radziszowski (see Links) periodically updates a survey with new results. See there for more information, known terms and bounds, and links to the relevant literature.
%C A401157 In his survey he uses the wheel graph definition W_n = K_1 + C_(n-1).
%H A401157 Stanisław Radziszowski, <a href="https://doi.org/10.37236/21">Small Ramsey Numbers</a>, The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics (see section 5.2).
%H A401157 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/RamseyNumber.html">Ramsey Number</a>.
%H A401157 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramsey%27s_theorem">Ramsey's theorem</a>.
%H A401157 <a href="/index/Ra#Ramsey">Index entries for sequences related to Ramsey numbers</a>.
%F A401157 T(n,k) = T(k,n).
%F A401157 T(3,k) = 2*k - 1 for k >= 6.
%e A401157 Array begins:
%e A401157   n\k|  3   4   5   6   7   8   9  10  ...
%e A401157   ----------------------------------------
%e A401157    3 |  6,  9, 11, 11, 13, 15, 17, 19, ... = essentially A005408
%e A401157    4 |  9, 18, 17, 19, 21,  ?,  ?,  ?, ...
%e A401157    5 | 11, 17, 15, 17, 15, 17, 18,  ?, ...
%e A401157    6 | 11, 19, 17, 17, 19,  ?,  ?,  ?, ...
%e A401157    7 | 13, 21, 15, 19, 19,  ?,  ?,  ?, ...
%e A401157    8 | 15,  ?, 17,  ?,  ?,  ?,  ?,  ?, ...
%e A401157    9 | 17,  ?, 18,  ?,  ?,  ?,  ?,  ?, ...
%e A401157   10 | 19,  ?,  ?,  ?,  ?,  ?,  ?,  ?, ...
%e A401157   ...                                \______ A401158 (main diagonal)
